.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-wallets-usage - What still names a wallet's address: payments as payee and payer, transactions sent


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin wallets usage  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Count what still names a wallet's address, across every account: the payments it is
the payee of, the payments it is the payer of, and the transactions it sent. How much
history the address carries: what 'rail0 admin wallets deactivate' shows before it asks.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for usage


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--account\fP=""
	The account the wallets belong to (required)

.PP
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin wallets usage 0x2D2B… --account 019e748b-…
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin-wallets(1)\fP
